WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.243
Dear Lord, look at this guy.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:21.030
The sculpted miracle of
a man is Bryan Johnson,

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.330
the very moneyed former owner of Venmo

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:28.713
who literally looks
younger every passing day.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.690
I consider myself to be

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:33.208
a professional rejuvenation athlete.

00:00:33.208 --> 00:00:37.803
The speed at which I'm aging
is less than a 10 year old.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.950
I spend millions of dollars a year.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.133
I do over a hundred
therapies in any given day.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.423
What Bryan really wants is to be 18 again,

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.150
and of course, so do I.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.843
We just seem to be going about
it in very different ways.

00:00:56.662 --> 00:00:58.443
I try to get perfect sleep.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.113
I eat 70 pounds of vegetables.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:14.493
I work out an hour a day.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.820
The science is still unclear

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.763
as to which of us will hit
the fountain of youth first,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.350
but the former Utahan thinks
part of the answer lies

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.840
within his newly claimed city.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.514
I love this place.

00:01:32.514 --> 00:01:35.490
When I was looking for where to live next

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.809
I mapped out a few big cities in the US.

00:01:37.809 --> 00:01:41.460
And I wanted to find something
where you could do and say

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:44.250
and be anything you wanted.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.050
And people a lot of times
think about LA Hollywood first.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:50.490
I think most people will be surprised

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.410
that there's like all this
other stuff going on here.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.210
There's this huge invisible world.

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:12.220
Los Angeles first called
to me when I was 18.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.970
I arrived here at the
spectacular Pomona College

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:22.683
where I was a philosophy major.

00:02:26.164 --> 00:02:27.747
Philosophy major.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.203
I am often asked, what
does a philosophy major do?

00:02:35.203 --> 00:02:38.250
Well, I do the only thing I can do,

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.863
which is show you people that can do.

00:02:47.121 --> 00:02:49.020
LA's thing has always been

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.033
that anything is possible here.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.580
Away from Hollywood fame,

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.001
it's a city rich with car culture,

00:02:59.001 --> 00:03:03.213
aerospace dreams and industrial factories.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.393
This is the LA that I love.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.750
And while you may not know it,

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.760
LA is in the midst of turning into

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.093
the most interesting
manufacturing hub in the world.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.168
To begin our journey into
this metallic underworld,

00:03:23.168 --> 00:03:26.749
let me show you Walden Speed Shop

00:03:26.749 --> 00:03:30.750
and its proprietor Bobby Walden.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.195
He's part of LA's manufacturing legacy.

00:03:33.195 --> 00:03:37.440
He's been in the metal
shaping industry for 35 years,

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.600
building and fixing vintage
car parts the old school way,

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:42.903
by hand.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.619
Metal shaping is an extremely old craft

00:03:47.619 --> 00:03:49.830
but the thing holds true

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.230
even from hundreds of years ago to now,

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.240
there's only three things
you can do to metal

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.840
and that's shrink it, and
stretch, it and form it,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.054
which is kind of folding.

00:03:59.054 --> 00:04:04.054
Other than that, there's
really no new technology.

00:04:04.231 --> 00:04:06.120
I really cut my teeth

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.126
making Harley Davidson
body parts from scratch.

00:04:09.126 --> 00:04:12.900
And then the hot rods
started working on those

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.417
and chopping tops and building
panels and fixing rust

00:04:16.417 --> 00:04:19.661
and then started building pieces of bodies

00:04:19.661 --> 00:04:23.553
and eventually became building
an entire body from scratch.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.300
If you're going to do what Bobby does,

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.210
Southern California is the place to do it.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.670
The cool thing about
Southern California first off

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:33.773
is the weather.

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:37.320
Back in the forties after World
War II, the guys came back.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.567
Now these guys are kids still.

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:43.410
And they have lived a life and seen things

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.404
that we can't even imagine today.

00:04:45.404 --> 00:04:48.098
And they come back, they're
alive, they're vibrant

00:04:48.098 --> 00:04:50.569
and they want thrills, right?

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.554
The first thing is they
start with the cars.

00:04:53.554 --> 00:04:55.560
They were cruising everywhere

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.110
and then they were probably
10 drag strips around here.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.990
You could drag race every day of the week

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.913
except for like Tuesday and Wednesday.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:11.103
It was 50 Mercurys and
the crazy custom cars.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:15.750
Because of the aerospace
industry that was out here,

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.240
there was a lot of available material

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:20.647
that you couldn't get
anywhere else in the country.

00:05:20.647 --> 00:05:22.530
The cars kept progressing

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.700
and getting better and better

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.112
and better and better, you know.

00:05:25.112 --> 00:05:27.397
Los Angeles at one time,

00:05:27.397 --> 00:05:31.736
there were thousands of shops
that did pattern making,

00:05:31.736 --> 00:05:33.877
small casting shops.

00:05:33.877 --> 00:05:36.344
And all these artisans,

00:05:36.344 --> 00:05:40.229
unbelievably gifted and
talented men and women.

00:05:40.229 --> 00:05:42.464
But as time evolved

00:05:42.464 --> 00:05:46.261
and manufacturing started
being sent overseas

00:05:46.261 --> 00:05:49.080
and automation came into play,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.165
then these skills and
these jobs and these shops

00:05:52.165 --> 00:05:54.829
got swallowed up and lost.

00:05:54.829 --> 00:05:56.940
If the trade or the skill

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.976
isn't taught and perpetuated forward,

00:05:59.976 --> 00:06:04.173
then we go back, we regress.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:10.740
Bobby offered to show me

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:13.170
every twist, bang and clang

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:16.890
that goes into making a simple metal bowl.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.000
If we're going to make a bowl,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.360
obviously this thing has to curl up.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.163
So it has to be shrunk.

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.730
He is a patient teacher.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.793
But it's real simple.
That gives you a tuck.

00:06:30.951 --> 00:06:32.520
So you kind of just
twisting back and forth.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:34.743
Yep, twist it like that,

00:06:35.776 --> 00:06:38.163
and then go back the other way.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.520
Is this a disaster?

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:42.363
No such thing.

00:06:48.023 --> 00:06:49.353
All right, perfect.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:54.437
Looks good. Serious.

00:06:55.362 --> 00:06:57.990
And now you go back in here

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.880
and you flatten these pooches out.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:00.713
Okay.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.630
This isn't a glamorous thing that I do.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:07.863
It's hard work.

00:07:09.126 --> 00:07:11.580
It's cold in the winter
and it's hot in the summer

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:13.713
and it's loud all the time.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.323
And when you go home at the
end of the day you are tired.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.020
If only we had a robot

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.969
that could pick up some of that slack.

00:07:29.969 --> 00:07:31.530
If you had a robot to pick
up the slack, you know,

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.164
and something you could
like scan something

00:07:34.164 --> 00:07:36.030
and it could memorize it,

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:37.770
keep the dimensions and all that stuff.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:38.603
Maybe one day.

00:07:38.603 --> 00:07:39.436
Maybe one day.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.523
Meanwhile, across town that very same day,

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.340
I am told that a group of robots

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.563
is sometimes known as a colony.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.190
And this colony of robots
belongs to Machina Labs

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:10.953
and it's co-founder and CEO, Ed Mehr.

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:16.260
These are the sheet metal
bending ultramodern successors

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.510
to folks like Bobby Walden.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:20.310
Sheet metal is the biggest
metal processing sector

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.230
out of all the metal processing.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:24.330
You pretty much, most of
the parts you see day to day

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:25.740
are sheet metal parts.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:26.910
You're driving in a freeway,

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:28.230
you're kind of in the CF sheet metal,

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:29.490
or you're sitting in an airplane,

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.208
it's basically a sheet metal canon.

00:08:31.208 --> 00:08:34.079
They don't really have
a very agile solution

00:08:34.079 --> 00:08:35.520
to make these parts.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:37.800
They all basically have
to build a factory for it

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:39.060
before you can get your
first batch of parts.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:40.200
Yeah.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:42.046
Factories that produce large metal parts,

00:08:42.046 --> 00:08:44.381
like car doors or airplane wings,

00:08:44.381 --> 00:08:48.275
can be the size of a football
field and three stories high,

00:08:48.275 --> 00:08:53.130
huge, very intricate and
not at all adaptable.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.870
This is just a common
problem manufacturing

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.308
that a lot of factories
are specifically built

00:08:57.308 --> 00:08:59.307
for the products they're
trying to manufacture.

00:08:59.307 --> 00:09:01.080
And the moment you want
to change your design,

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:01.913
you want to change your material,

00:09:01.913 --> 00:09:04.143
you have to almost build another factory.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:06.660
So what we're trying
to do at Machina really

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.320
is can we have that overarching automation

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:11.670
that is not process specific.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:13.138
It's a platform that allows the robot

00:09:13.138 --> 00:09:16.260
to go pick up the right tool
and apply it to the material

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:17.640
so they can get the right part,

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:19.066
the same way a craftsman or blacksmith

00:09:19.066 --> 00:09:20.823
used to do these parts.

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:26.250
These bots have the skills

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:29.130
to make fancy new shapes and old ones

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:32.313
that are too challenging for
other companies to make today.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.960
I know a lot of the stuff you work on

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.190
is kind of top secret.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:40.290
Can we talk about all these things?

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:41.123
Yeah.

00:09:41.123 --> 00:09:41.956
Yeah.

00:09:41.956 --> 00:09:43.328
This one's an interesting one.

00:09:43.328 --> 00:09:47.850
It's actually a composite
mold for a drone wing.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.037
So they basically lay up
composite on top of this

00:09:50.037 --> 00:09:53.188
and then they put it in a vacuum
bag and put it in a furnace

00:09:53.188 --> 00:09:55.438
and then they get a
composite part out of it.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:59.070
Is this your donut operation?

00:09:59.070 --> 00:09:59.903
Yeah, I know.

00:09:59.903 --> 00:10:01.410
Giant donut.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:02.340
This is actually like,

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:05.610
Toroidal Tanks is one of the
most good designs for like,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.260
you know, satellites tanks.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:09.330
Okay, like a fuel tank or-

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:10.201
Fuel tanks.
Okay.

00:10:10.201 --> 00:10:11.850
One of the challenges with these

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.770
is that it's very hard to manufacture.

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:16.526
Back in the sixties actually,
NASA used to make these things

00:10:16.526 --> 00:10:21.526
and they mostly relied on basically
talented individuals. - People
we've like lost the knowledge-

00:10:23.768 --> 00:10:24.900
NASA knowledge.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:26.220
And so we're kind of working with NASA

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.820
to revive some of these as well.

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:30.450
But even if you're not NASA,

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.930
just a regular Joe or Jane metal fanatic,

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:37.203
you too can hire a pair of
these robots to do your bidding.

00:10:39.806 --> 00:10:43.860
This is your mocking a starter
pack if you're a customer.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:47.820
You get two robots and you get
this platform in the center

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:49.800
that holds the metal sheets

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:53.254
that costs you about $2.5
million to get going.

00:10:53.254 --> 00:10:55.710
And then it's about learning this process

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:59.430
of orchestrating the robots
using Machina software

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:01.263
and getting going with these things.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:07.827
To truly put these robots to the test,

00:11:07.827 --> 00:11:12.453
I had what's known in LA as
the engineer's skin peel.

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.300
This is step one of having
your face built by a robot

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:21.060
is to get this mug scanned

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:23.910
with a laser that's
also mounted on a robot.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.190
It's not super dangerous.
It's a class two laser.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:28.090
It's like one level above a laser pointer.

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:30.482
Probably want to close
your eyes when we do this?

00:11:30.482 --> 00:11:31.786
Ready?

00:11:31.786 --> 00:11:32.700
Ready.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.470
Alright.
Please tell it to be gentle.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:35.880
Alright. Eyes closed.

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:37.323
3, 2, 1. Here we go.

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:44.853
After the laser, well, lasered my face,

00:11:44.853 --> 00:11:49.853
the resulting 3D file was sent
to these two fine workers.

00:11:50.046 --> 00:11:53.820
They set about making metal me,

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:56.580
each with its own clearly defined role.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.070
At each point in time is
basically one robot is forming,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:00.930
and the other robot we
call like a support robot.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:01.763
Okay.

00:12:01.763 --> 00:12:04.200
So while one robot puts all its effort

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.210
into deforming the sheet of metal,

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.205
the other robot makes
sure that deformation

00:12:08.205 --> 00:12:10.833
is only happening where they want it to.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:14.010
In this case, on the indent,

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.923
increasingly looking like someone I know.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:23.823
It's just like me.

00:12:26.053 --> 00:12:28.350
Good job, robot. Good job.

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.240
And the robot generated wonders

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:31.503
didn't and there.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.460
So Ashlee, we made a little piece for you.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.860
Kind of take as a souvenir from LA.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:38.897
Yeah, it was awesome.

00:12:38.897 --> 00:12:43.897
I feel like the flavor
flavor of robot machinery.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.378
Yeah, it looks good on you.

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:48.090
That is awesome man. Thank you.

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:49.953
I feel I've made it.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:53.133
Admiring my glorious metal bounty,

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.067
I couldn't help but wonder,

00:12:57.067 --> 00:13:02.010
what is it about LA that makes
such innovation possible?

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:03.600
Every time I come to LA,

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.360
it's this new take on manufacturing

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:09.201
and this way a lot of it
comes out of aerospace.

00:13:09.201 --> 00:13:10.878
But to me it feels like

00:13:10.878 --> 00:13:15.030
if there is a manufacturing
revolution taking place

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:16.835
where all this stuff works

00:13:16.835 --> 00:13:18.210
and we make some kind of
step change to a new age,

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:21.000
it feels like LA is somehow ended up

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.800
as the epicenter of that.

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:25.898
Is this like coincidence
or this had to be here?

00:13:25.898 --> 00:13:27.270
No, I agree with you.

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:31.383
And honestly like partially
it's because of SpaceX.

00:13:32.382 --> 00:13:35.370
It's hard to talk about
LA's manufacturing scene

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.809
without acknowledging
the Elon in the room.

00:13:38.809 --> 00:13:42.570
Like just about everyone
here doing sci-fi building,

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:44.490
Ed used to work at SpaceX,

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:48.840
the aerospace giant located on
the outskirts of Los Angeles.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:50.490
I think one thing Elon did

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:51.900
for the folks who went to SpaceX was like

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:55.020
he really broke that fear
of testing with hardware.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:56.700
It was like, well why
don't we just make hardware

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:57.780
the same way you make software?

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:58.613
Yeah.

00:13:58.613 --> 00:14:00.510
Like why don't you just test it

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.280
and you know, every two
months, three months

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.322
try something else.

00:14:03.322 --> 00:14:06.000
And a lot of folks who
were exposed to that,

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.250
I think got a little
bit of confidence there.

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:09.150
It was like, okay, yeah, no,

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:12.450
I can put together a
facility with the 22 robots

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:13.290
and just figure this out.

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:14.123
Yeah.

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.536
Next stop, another former SpaceX-er

00:14:20.536 --> 00:14:23.913
taking manufacturing to the stars.

00:14:37.909 --> 00:14:40.440
When I was sitting at
SpaceX working on the plan

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:41.831
for how we're going to colonize Mars,

00:14:41.831 --> 00:14:45.018
my brain kept coming back
to over and over again,

00:14:45.018 --> 00:14:47.463
how are we going to make things on Mars?

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:52.544
We're not going to ship
billet and machine it on Mars.

00:14:52.544 --> 00:14:55.693
We'll want to make it in
situ while we're there.

00:14:55.693 --> 00:14:58.410
That also kind of planted the seed for me

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:00.695
to kind of go into the 3D printing.

00:15:00.695 --> 00:15:04.477
Erik Palitsch, co-founder
and CEO of Freeform

00:15:04.477 --> 00:15:07.069
wants to make building size 3D printers

00:15:07.069 --> 00:15:10.165
with conveyor belts and assembly lines.

00:15:10.165 --> 00:15:13.871
And to do that, they got to
have a crap ton of lasers

00:15:13.871 --> 00:15:16.923
and the digital brains
to coordinate them all.

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:19.773
He's working on a prototype.

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:21.533
Alright, so this is the machine.

00:15:21.533 --> 00:15:22.413
This is it.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:24.990
Lovingly call it golden eye.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:26.562
Yeah, 'cause of the lasers.

00:15:26.562 --> 00:15:28.293
Yes, because of the lasers.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:32.430
The lasers are what make

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:34.211
metal 3D printing possible,

00:15:34.211 --> 00:15:35.920
heating up a metallic powder

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:39.660
and welding each new layer
to the previous layer.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:42.660
We have more lasers on a
system that's running right now

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:44.684
while we're talking than
anyone in the world.

00:15:44.684 --> 00:15:47.790
Okay, let's have a look.

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:49.380
I just-

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:51.000
Let me get you a little-

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.860
Can I have a stool please?

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:56.137
My viewing stool.
The viewing stool, yeah.

00:15:56.137 --> 00:15:58.901
Yeah, it is crazy.

00:15:58.901 --> 00:16:02.520
You could just see all the
lasers got to go at once right?

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.517
Yeah, all 18 should be going right now.

00:16:04.517 --> 00:16:06.330
It looks like something

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:08.190
engineered by a super villain

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:11.043
to kill a secret agent all too slowly.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:15.420
But this machine represents

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:18.210
a huge breakthrough for 3D printing.

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:20.089
It's much faster, much cheaper

00:16:20.089 --> 00:16:23.730
and much more precise
than what has come before.

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:26.130
It possibly gets 3D printing close

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:29.040
to being mass manufacturing technology.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:30.810
There has been this belief for many years

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:34.353
that the only way to go faster
is to trade trade quality.

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:38.640
The real problem is that
the machines today are,

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.863
for lack of a better word, are dumb.

00:16:40.863 --> 00:16:43.170
You pre-program instructions

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:45.057
and then you tell the
machine to go do that.

00:16:45.057 --> 00:16:47.550
You just iterate on it
until you get it right

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:48.510
and then don't change it.

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:50.681
What a shame for a technology
that shouldn't care

00:16:50.681 --> 00:16:52.950
what the part is coming off the machine.

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:53.783
Right.

00:16:53.783 --> 00:16:55.566
I mean the whole thing is about
creativity and imagination.

00:16:55.566 --> 00:16:56.460
Exactly.

00:16:56.460 --> 00:16:57.946
Sort of limitlessness.

00:16:57.946 --> 00:17:00.630
Freeform un-dumbs these contraptions

00:17:00.630 --> 00:17:03.510
by collecting information
on what the machine did

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:05.730
and feeding that into a neural network

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:08.520
that can then learn how
to do it better next time,

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:12.960
a process that requires an
unholy amount of information.

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:14.130
It's actually kind of crazy,

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:17.940
the amount of data we move here
is like rivals like Netflix,

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:21.886
you know, like the
sheer magnitude of data.

00:17:21.886 --> 00:17:24.120
All that data means Freeform

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:28.207
can make insanely complex
designs out of robust metal,

00:17:28.207 --> 00:17:31.591
and eventually rocket engines, car parts

00:17:31.591 --> 00:17:34.413
and whatever it is they make on Mars.

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:42.327
At this point in my journey
through Los Angeles,

00:17:42.327 --> 00:17:46.460
I was feeling that like any
other TV host in Hollywood,

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:50.051
I needed a useless award for myself.

00:17:50.051 --> 00:17:52.233
So I had Erik print one up.

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:55.516
For exploring manufacturing in Los Angeles

00:17:55.516 --> 00:17:57.478
and doing such an amazing job,

00:17:57.478 --> 00:17:59.943
I present to you the Worldy.

00:18:02.993 --> 00:18:05.054
Thank you so much.

00:18:05.054 --> 00:18:06.673
This is glorious.

00:18:06.673 --> 00:18:08.973
What's it made out of?

00:18:08.973 --> 00:18:09.873
Metal.

00:18:11.903 --> 00:18:13.149
That's incredible.

00:18:13.149 --> 00:18:15.395
Thank you. Thank you.

00:18:15.395 --> 00:18:16.833
Thank you.

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:23.700
Seeing what marvels can be accomplished

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.755
without the touch of a human hand,

00:18:25.755 --> 00:18:30.630
I couldn't help but think of
the Bobby Waldens of the world.

00:18:30.630 --> 00:18:33.900
The nerd in me just likes
the machines themselves

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:35.760
and seeing what comes out of them.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:38.610
But like none of this
stuff comes with many jobs.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.512
So there is this like
kind of hollow feeling

00:18:41.512 --> 00:18:45.525
that accompanies this, even
if it's a huge success.

00:18:45.525 --> 00:18:47.280
We're trying to take the human

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:49.650
out of the loop of the process itself

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:51.179
'cause humans aren't good at that.

00:18:51.179 --> 00:18:53.160
But we're going to need lots of people

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:54.870
to build these factories.

00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:57.510
We're going to need people
to maintain these factories.

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:00.501
Computers cannot substitute
for human creativity.

00:19:00.501 --> 00:19:02.160
That's what we should be
training people to do,

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:04.014
to like deploy these precision robotics

00:19:04.014 --> 00:19:06.030
and maintain these precision robotics,

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:08.880
you know, not like vacuum
powder off a plate, you know.

00:19:11.555 --> 00:19:14.613
Come on buddy. Here we go.

00:19:14.613 --> 00:19:15.780
Let's go home.

00:19:18.683 --> 00:19:22.336
Carrying around all this metal
was becoming a real drag.

00:19:22.336 --> 00:19:26.277
So I skipped across town
for something a bit lighter,

00:19:26.277 --> 00:19:29.934
sugar at a company that's invented

00:19:29.934 --> 00:19:33.003
a whole new way of making sweet treats.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:36.540
Super proud of this.

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:37.935
This is the current 3D printer,

00:19:37.935 --> 00:19:41.373
we designed and built it from scratch.

00:19:42.849 --> 00:19:44.400
You can see like it looks like

00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:46.190
a kind of commercial kitchen equipment.

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:50.430
But it also contains all of the processes

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:52.530
that we're familiar with with 3D printing.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:57.797
It manages this dry powder,
which is starch and sugar.

00:19:57.797 --> 00:20:00.480
And then we've got a print head over here

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.363
which moves across and
actually prints the object.

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:07.170
These 3D printers can go
into any commercial kitchen

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:10.318
in the US and even across the globe.

00:20:10.318 --> 00:20:12.690
Very cool. And I could just eat it?

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:13.523
Yeah.

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:19.860
I don't recommend eating
that all in one sitting.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:21.909
But yes, everything is edible,

00:20:21.909 --> 00:20:24.180
completely edible.
Flying off the walls.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:25.650
For the taste test.

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:27.506
So we have a nice assortment
of different things.

00:20:27.506 --> 00:20:30.990
Chef Jonathan walked me through the menu.

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:32.700
We have dealer's choice here.

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:34.295
I got some chocolate covered sushi,

00:20:34.295 --> 00:20:38.947
or maybe something a little
more refreshing, like our lime.

00:20:38.947 --> 00:20:41.020
Like a skull.
Skull.

00:20:41.020 --> 00:20:42.690
Just your classic skull.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:45.270
Just your classic experience skull.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:47.999
Hmm. Oh yeah, you could taste the lime.

00:20:47.999 --> 00:20:49.050
Yeah.

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:50.250
It's sweet, but it's not as sweet

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:51.780
as I kind of thought it was going to be.

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:53.790
It's like more meringue like in texture.

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:54.623
That is what I was looking for.

00:20:54.623 --> 00:20:56.220
It's like a meringue.

00:20:56.220 --> 00:20:58.920
But Sugar Lab isn't limited to sugar.

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:01.776
As long as the food can
be dehydrated into powder,

00:21:01.776 --> 00:21:03.483
the team can print it.

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:09.133
Think matcha, think kimchi,
think sparkly stuff.

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:13.560
We do a lot of drink drops dissolvables

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:16.650
that we put in our
cocktails, wine, champagnes.

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:17.726
So you just plunk it?

00:21:17.726 --> 00:21:20.700
Oh, it's cool. You get the
color and you get the fizz.

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:22.050
Maybe give it a little stir.

00:21:22.050 --> 00:21:22.883
Can I taste?

00:21:22.883 --> 00:21:23.716
Yeah.

00:21:23.716 --> 00:21:25.560
Is it safe to drink this much glitter?

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:26.393
Yeah. Yeah.

00:21:26.393 --> 00:21:29.220
It's totally food safe edible glitter.

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:31.110
But there was more.

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:33.395
We have a strawberry covered chocolate.

00:21:33.395 --> 00:21:34.944
And more.

00:21:34.944 --> 00:21:37.320
Here we got our everlasting gobstopper.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:38.190
And more.

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:39.950
A cranberry Frankenstein.

00:21:39.950 --> 00:21:42.480
One of our nineties bon bon shells.

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:43.590
A matcha Buddha for you.

00:21:43.590 --> 00:21:45.150
Hazy IPA ganache.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:46.470
Bon Bon or glitter salt.

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:47.850
They're happy little pencil bon bon.

00:21:47.850 --> 00:21:49.230
You got our fun little octopus there.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.000
Your favorite boat drink drops.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:51.833
It sparkles.

00:21:51.833 --> 00:21:53.250
The green tea bon bon.

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:54.300
That's really good.

00:21:56.190 --> 00:21:57.791
I don't recommend eating that.

00:21:57.791 --> 00:21:59.733
Don't recommend eating those.

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:03.690
The thing about LA, man, is
that the ideas are so big.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:04.770
You can do anything here.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:06.210
You can print things out of sugar.

00:22:06.210 --> 00:22:07.583
You can buy Twitter
with your spare change.

00:22:07.583 --> 00:22:10.623
You can mine asteroids.
You can go to Mars.

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:16.293
Sugar Ashlee is right.

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:22.443
In this city, the galaxy
is truly your oyster.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:25.203
But how do you get there?

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:29.613
Welcome to LA Traffic.

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:37.293
Well, that's next time.

